Wireless sensing has shown significant convenience and privacy-preserving sensing capabilities. However, its practical deployment is hindered by the scarcity of high-quality labeled radio frequency (RF) signals necessary for training. This paper aims to solve this dilemma by developing a video-to-RF signal mapping framework. The key idea is to utilize everyday short videos (e.g., clips recorded by smartphones or activity logs from home security cameras) to learn a general mapping model for synthesizing high-quality wireless signals for training. Realizing such a video-to-RF mapping is challenging because, while both modalities capture electromagnetic waves (i.e., visible light and RF signals), the wavelength discrepancy restricts video to primarily capture reflections, whereas RF signals comprise both reflections and diffractions. To bridge this modality gap, we design a framework that synthesizes RF signals using geometrical theories of diffraction and reflection, leveraging 3D human shapes, the spatial position of RF devices, and material information of surrounding objects within video streams. A 3D U-Net model is built to map the diffraction and reflection representations derived from videos into RF signals. Furthermore, we design a multi-domain meta-learning approach to encourage the framework to learn a generalizable mapping (e.g., when converting videos of new users and environments into RF signals). Comprehensive evaluations on WiFi sensing show that our framework synthesizes realistic signals capable of training robust models for various WiFi sensing applications, achieving recognition accuracies of over 88% for unseen users, 80% for unseen activities, and 70% for unseen environments, without requiring real RF data for training.